			)
		)
		(uuid "943e4062-68f3-4537-9d05-d6e2eaaeb103")
	)
	(text "How the 15× stage is biased despite AC coupling and no VOCM resistors\nHere's the part that actually answers your original question. The populated stage has no explicit R44/R49-style bias resistors — and it doesn't need them, because the feedback resistors do that job:\n\nThe internal VOCM loop holds both outputs at VOCM (≈1.65 V) at DC.\nR40/R52 (8.2 k) connect each output back to its summing junction.\nThe R_G leg (R46/R47 = 550 Ω) is DC-blocked by C158/C164, so no DC current flows through it.\nWith no current in R_G, there's no DC drop, so each summing junction floats up to VOCM through R_F. Input CM = VOCM automatically.\n\nThat's a standard, textbook AC-coupled FDA — ADI's own datasheet application circuit relies on R_F to set input CM this way. So the \"inconsistency\" resolves cleanly: the 15× stage is biased through its feedback resistors, and the explicit VOCM resistors are simply unnecessary when R_F is a clean DC path.\n\n\nWhy the DNP stage still shows R44/R49 to VOCM\nThe DNP second stage (U35A2A) has the more elaborate feedback footprint — R39 ∥ a series R-C branch (C155/C156, etc.) for pole/zero shaping. \nThe moment a feedback path could be DC-blocked (a cap in series in the feedback), R_F no longer guarantees a DC path to the summing node, and you'd need R44/R49 to pin the input CM to VOCM. \nSo the DNP footprint carries those bias resistors as insurance for the flexible configuration; the simple 15× stage with a pure-resistive R_F doesn't. "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 263.906 319.786 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "9bc4f024-b306-4043-9a56-20a5e2f5718a")
	)
